  "hero.title": "How Nginx Handles Ten Thousand Connections",
  "hero.kicker": "Computing · Software principles · A hands-on long essay",
  "hero.sub": "A dozen lines of `nginx.conf`, and you type `nginx` to launch.<br>Facing ten thousand concurrent connections, why does a thread-per-connection server collapse while a handful of single-threaded Nginx workers stay calm?",
  "hero.lead": "A modern web server often holds tens of thousands of open HTTP Keep-Alive sockets at once, most of them idle during any given millisecond while waiting for the next request. If you handle that concurrency by spawning one operating-system thread per incoming TCP socket, passing ten thousand concurrent clients (the classic C10K problem) overwhelms virtual stack reservations, CPU context-switching, and accept locks long before your network card runs out of bandwidth. This essay asks one question: <strong>once a minimal `nginx.conf` starts up, how do a few mostly lock-free single-threaded worker processes watch ten thousand sockets at once, pick exact routes, smooth upstream bursts, enforce millisecond rate limits, and reload config with zero dropped connections?</strong>",
  "hero.note": "The data structures, constants, and core algorithms here follow <strong>Nginx 1.28.0 stable</strong> (released April 23, 2025) across `src/core/`, `src/event/`, and `src/http/`, with Chapter 10 tracking newer stable and mainline tracks through November 2026. Because no native `nginx` binary is installed on this build host, every interactive model rewrites the exact C rules in client-side JavaScript and is checked by `tools/crosscheck.cjs` against closed-form scaling laws, invariant identities, and spec edge cases (recorded in `BRIEF.md`). For prerequisites, see <a href=\"../../systems/how-the-os-talks-to-hardware/\">How the OS Talks to Hardware</a> (syscalls and `epoll`), <a href=\"../../systems/how-virtual-memory-works/\">How Virtual Memory Works</a> (virtual address spaces and copy-on-write `fork()`), <a href=\"../../systems/how-a-nic-moves-packets/\">How a Network Card Moves Packets</a> (multi-queue NICs and DMA), and <a href=\"../../../variants/claude-code/tcp-congestion/\">Network Flow · A Message’s Journey</a> (TCP handshakes and bandwidth-delay). Each chapter ends by stating what the model leaves out.",
  "ch1.title": "The C10K cliff: why one thread per connection collapses",
  "toc.ch1": "The C10K cliff: stack & context switches",
  "ch1.law": "At ten thousand concurrent clients, the first wall you hit is not wire bandwidth—it is the memory bill and scheduler thrashing of binding an OS thread to every socket. Nginx flips the design: one single-threaded worker process per CPU core, reducing each idle socket to a few hundred bytes in a flat array.",
  "ch1.p1": "Here is a minimal working `nginx.conf`. The top directives launch 4 worker processes holding up to 4,096 connections each, listening on port 80. Starting `nginx` boots the workers; editing a route later takes one `nginx -s reload`.",
  "ch1.code.aria": "Minimal nginx.conf and hot reload command",
  "ch1.code": "worker_processes 4;\nevents {\n    worker_connections 4096;\n}\nhttp {\n    server {\n        listen 80 reuseport;\n        location = / { return 200 \"ok\"; }\n        location /static/ { root /var/www; }\n    }\n}\n# Hot reload config: nginx -s reload",
  "ch1.p2": "Serving 10,000 Keep-Alive clients with one POSIX thread per connection hits Linux's default `8 MiB` virtual thread stack limit—reserving `78.13 GiB` of virtual address space just for stacks (counting roughly 128 KiB of touched stack pages, kernel `task_struct`, and thread-local heap per thread, even 5% active traffic consumes around `1,281 MiB` of resident RAM). Worse still is scheduling: as those 500 active threads repeatedly block and wake on socket reads and writes across 4 CPU cores, millions of context switches per second flush L1 instruction/data caches and TLBs; at 3.5 microseconds per switch, scheduler overhead alone burns `87.5%` of available core time.",
  "ch1.p3": "Nginx breaks the link between connection count and thread count. Inside `ngx_event_process_init`, each worker preallocates three contiguous arrays sized to `worker_connections`: `ngx_connection_t` (~`232` bytes on 64-bit Linux), a read `ngx_event_t` (`104` bytes), and a write `ngx_event_t` (`104` bytes). An idle socket needs zero threads and only `440` bytes; only the 500 actively executing requests borrow a 4 KiB request pool, totaling `6.15 MiB`—roughly `208` times less resident memory, with virtually zero cross-thread core migration.",
  "dg.c10k.cap": "Figure 1. Thread-per-connection vs Nginx single-threaded workers at 10,000 connections (64-bit Linux struct sizes).",
  "dg.c10k.aria": "Diagram: top red block for thread-per-connection showing 8 MiB stacks and L1 thrashing; bottom cyan block for 4 Nginx workers holding 440-byte connection slots",
