  "ch1.say.collapse": "<em>C10K collapse zone</em>: {act} active requests among {n} sockets trigger constant wakeups, burning <em>{ctx}</em> of core capacity on thread switches; Nginx holds the lot in {nrss} without thread churn.",
  "ch1.p4": "To see why `78.13 GiB` of virtual stack reservations does not crash a 16 GiB host until threads actually touch stack pages, read <a href=\"../../systems/how-virtual-memory-works/#ch1\">How Virtual Memory Works, Chapters 1 and 7</a>.",
  "ch1.limit": "The model leaves out: each worker's fixed binary and shared-library footprint (a few MiB), TLS session state memory, and hybrid user-space coroutine or thread-pool designs that lower stack sizes while still paying user-level context-switch costs. Switching cost is a parameterized 4-core estimate (3.5 µs per switch) intended to show scaling order.",
  "ch2.title": "Master and Worker: how zero-downtime hot reload hands off sockets",
  "toc.ch2": "Master–Worker & hot reload",
  "ch2.law": "Nginx splits control from data: the Master validates config, holds the listening sockets, and routes signals, never touching a client byte; on reload, old and new worker generations share the same listening file descriptor to hand over traffic with zero dropped handshakes.",
  "ch2.p1": "Changing a routing rule in production without severing an ongoing download rests on the Master–Worker split. On startup, the privileged Master binds `:80` and `:443` (say file descriptor `fd=6`) and calls `fork()` to spawn workers, which inherit the open kernel socket entry directly.",
  "ch2.p2": "When you run `nginx -s reload` (sending `SIGHUP` to the Master), five steps run in order: the Master syntax-checks the new config, opens fresh log descriptors, `fork()`s a <strong>new worker generation (v2)</strong> that immediately inherits `fd=6` and starts calling `accept()` on new TCP handshakes, and finally sends `NGX_CMD_QUIT` to the <strong>old workers (v1)</strong>. Each old worker unregisters `fd=6` from its event loop—refusing new handshakes—yet keeps serving every in-flight response until its last socket closes, exiting cleanly one by one. Full binary upgrades extend the same inheritance trick using `USR2` (launch new Master), `WINCH` (drain old workers), and `QUIT`.",
  "dg.reload.cap": "Figure 2. Seamless SIGHUP handoff of listening socket FD=6 between draining v1 workers and newly forked v2 workers.",
  "dg.reload.aria": "Diagram: top gold Master holding FD=6 sends QUIT to dashed red v1 workers to drain existing traffic while forking green v2 workers inheriting FD=6 for new handshakes",
