  "ch2.say.done": "t = {t} ms (handoff complete): all old workers have exited cleanly and {newW} v2 workers run the new config—<em>0</em> connections severed end to end.",
  "ch2.say.hard": "<em>Hard restart drops traffic</em> (t = {t} ms): killing workers severs 20 active connections immediately and rejects new handshakes during the listener gap, losing <em>{drop}</em> connections.",
  "ch2.p3": "At the default snapshot (`t = 1,200 ms` with a `1,200 ms` tail), the 2 faster v1 workers have already exited leaving `2` draining, while all `4` v2 workers have been accepting new traffic since `500 ms`; a hard restart has already dropped `26` connections. How `fork()` spins up v2 workers instantly without copying process RAM upfront is covered in <a href=\"../../systems/how-virtual-memory-works/#ch9\">How Virtual Memory Works, Chapter 9 (Copy-on-Write)</a>.",
  "ch2.limit": "The model leaves out: forced `worker_shutdown_timeout` cutoffs, environment variable `NGINX` FD passing during `USR2` binary upgrades, and arbitrary long-lived WebSocket or streaming tails that keep a draining worker alive for minutes.",
  "ch3.title": "Non-blocking event loop: how `epoll` and `SO_REUSEPORT` watch 10,000 sockets in O(active)",
  "toc.ch3": "epoll event loop & thundering herd",
  "ch3.law": "Out of ten thousand open connections, often only a few dozen send or receive bytes during any single millisecond. `select` and `poll` rescan all ten thousand slots every tick, whereas `epoll_wait` hands back only the short linked list of ready descriptors.",
  "ch3.p1": "With every socket marked non-blocking (`O_NONBLOCK`), each Nginx worker's `ngx_process_events_and_timers` loop is tiny: compute the nearest timer deadline, sleep inside `epoll_wait` (or `kqueue` on BSD/macOS), and wake when NIC interrupts deliver data and push ready `epitem` structs onto the kernel's `rdllist`. The worker processes only those `k` active events and loops straight back.",
  "ch3.p2": "At 10,000 Keep-Alive sockets with 40 active per millisecond, `select`/`poll` must copy a 10,000-bit `fd_set` bitmap across the user/kernel boundary and scan every slot `O(N)`, taking roughly `160.8` µs in the parameterized model; `epoll` pops only the 40 ready nodes `O(k)` in `10.8` µs—inspecting `250` times fewer descriptors.",
  "ch3.p3": "Sharing one listening socket across multiple workers introduces a second snag: the <strong>thundering herd</strong>. Older kernels woke every sleeping worker on a single incoming SYN, letting one succeed in `accept()` while three wasted a wakeup. Nginx originally arbitrated access with `accept_mutex`; since version 1.11.3, modern Linux kernels provide single-wakeup `EPOLLEXCLUSIVE` (`NGX_EXCLUSIVE_EVENT`), allowing `accept_mutex` to default to `off`. Adding `listen 80 reuseport;` goes one step further: the kernel allocates a separate listening socket and accept queue per worker and hashes incoming 4-tuples straight to one core, eliminating shared accept-queue lock contention altogether.",
  "dg.epoll.cap": "Figure 3. select/poll scanning all O(N) slots every tick vs epoll returning O(active) ready descriptors and SO_REUSEPORT per-worker queues.",
  "dg.epoll.aria": "Diagram: top red box select/poll scanning 10,000 bits per tick; middle cyan box epoll/kqueue pushing 40 ready descriptors into rdllist; bottom gold box SO_REUSEPORT per-worker accept queues",
