  "ch3.say.shared": "Shared queue without exclusion: with {k} active out of {n} sockets, epoll cuts scan cost from {sel} to {ep} ({ratio}× fewer checks), yet each SYN wakes all <em>{wake} workers</em> to fight over one lock.",
  "ch3.say.mutex": "accept_mutex or EPOLLEXCLUSIVE: epoll handles {k} active events ({ep} vs {sel}) and wakes only <em>1 worker</em> per SYN, ending the herd while still sharing 1 kernel queue.",
  "ch3.say.reuseport": "<em>SO_REUSEPORT</em>: epoll scans {k} active events ({ep}) while 4 independent kernel listen queues hash incoming SYNs straight to 1 target worker with zero accept-queue lock contention.",
  "ch3.p4": "For how kernel interrupts feed `epoll`'s ready list and how NIC Receive Side Scaling (RSS) spreads packets across cores, see <a href=\"../../systems/how-the-os-talks-to-hardware/#ch11\">How the OS Talks to Hardware, Chapter 11</a> and <a href=\"../../systems/how-a-nic-moves-packets/#ch5\">How a Network Card Moves Packets, Chapter 5</a>.",
  "ch3.limit": "The model leaves out: edge-triggered `EPOLLET` looping until `EAGAIN` (which Nginx uses by default on `epoll`), hardware interrupt coalescing delays, and temporary socket-hash imbalance when reloading under `SO_REUSEPORT` (addressed in Chapter 10).",
  "ch4.title": "The 11-phase HTTP state machine and `location` precedence",
  "toc.ch4": "11-phase HTTP pipeline & location routing",
  "ch4.law": "Nginx does not execute `nginx.conf` top-to-bottom like a shell script. Every request marches through `11` fixed `ngx_http_phases`, and in `FIND_CONFIG` a `location` block's rank comes from hard modifier rules rather than file order.",
  "ch4.p1": "In `src/http/ngx_http_core_module.h`, HTTP execution is fixed across `11` stages: `POST_READ`, `SERVER_REWRITE`, `FIND_CONFIG` (picking the `location`), `REWRITE`, `POST_REWRITE` (looping back to `FIND_CONFIG` if a rewrite mutated the URI), `PREACCESS` (where `limit_req` and `limit_conn` execute), `ACCESS` (`allow`/`deny` and auth), `POST_ACCESS`, `PRECONTENT` (`try_files`), `CONTENT` (serving files or `proxy_pass`), and `LOG`. Even if you write `allow 10.0.0.1;` above `limit_req` in your config file, rate limiting still runs before access control because `PREACCESS` always precedes `ACCESS`.",
  "ch4.p2": "Inside `FIND_CONFIG` (`ngx_http_core_find_location`), Nginx compiles static prefixes into a balanced binary search tree at startup and resolves routes in four strict steps:",
  "ch4.s1": "<strong>Exact match (`=`)</strong>: Checked first. If `location = /` matches the URI verbatim, Nginx locks it immediately and halts.",
  "ch4.s2": "<strong>Longest static prefix check (`^~` vs plain)</strong>: Searches the prefix tree for the <strong>longest matching prefix</strong>. If that single longest winning prefix carries `^~` (`noregexp`), Nginx locks it and <strong>skips all regular expressions</strong>; otherwise it saves that prefix as a fallback candidate.",
  "ch4.s3": "**Regular expressions in config order (`~` case-sensitive, `~*` case-insensitive)<strong>: Evaluated top-to-bottom as written; the </strong>first** match wins immediately.",
  "ch4.s4": "<strong>Longest-prefix fallback</strong>: If every regex misses, Nginx falls back to the saved longest prefix from Step 2.",
  "ch4.p3": "Here is the classic production trap in Step 2: suppose you configure `location ^~ /static/` (length 8) alongside `location /static/images/` (length 15, missing `^~`). When `/static/images/pic.jpg` arrives, the prefix tree picks `/static/images/` because 15 > 8; since that longest winner lacks `^~`, Nginx still proceeds to evaluate regexes—letting `~ \\.(png|jpg|js)$` hijack the route!",
  "dg.phases.cap": "Figure 4. The 11 ngx_http_phases and the rewrite loop returning to FIND_CONFIG.",
  "dg.phases.aria": "Diagram: top-to-bottom six phase groups from 0 POST_READ to 10 LOG, showing location precedence in phase 2 and the violet rewrite loop back from 3-4 to 2",
