  "ch4.say.prefix_fallback": "Exact and regex checks all missed <code>{uri}</code>, so Step 4 fell back to the longest prefix <em>{win}</em> saved in Step 2.",
  "ch4.p4": "Try picking `/static/logo.png` with `^~ /static/` enabled (it halts regexes and stays in `/static/`), then switch to `/static/images/pic.jpg`: even with `^~ /static/` still checked, `/static/images/` wins the longest-prefix tree and hands control straight to the regex!",
  "ch4.limit": "The model leaves out: automatic trailing-slash 301 redirects (`NGX_DONE`), internal jumps to named `@location` blocks, and the 10-hop internal redirect guard (`limit_internal_redirects`).",
  "ch5.title": "Per-request memory pools and buffer chains",
  "toc.ch5": "ngx_pool_t & buffer chains",
  "ch5.law": "To keep small header allocations from shattering the heap through thousands of `malloc()` and `free()` calls per second, Nginx hands each request a contiguous `ngx_pool_t` arena, bump-allocating objects with pointer addition and reclaiming everything at once when the request ends.",
  "ch5.p1": "Parsing one HTTP request touches dozens of tiny strings—URI slices, header names, cookies, upstream buffers. Calling general-purpose `malloc()` and `free()` for each piece pays allocator lock/cache overhead and slowly fragments memory over weeks of uptime.",
  "ch5.p2": "In `src/core/ngx_palloc.h`, Nginx cuts allocation down to moving one pointer. A newly created pool reserves `80` bytes at the front for its `ngx_pool_t` header (`NGX_DEFAULT_POOL_SIZE` is `16 KiB`, while individual HTTP requests typically allocate 4 KiB blocks), placing `d.last` at the start of free bytes and `d.end` at the block boundary. Small allocations cap at `pool->max` (`4096 - 80 = 4,016` bytes in a 4 KiB pool, and never above `NGX_MAX_ALLOC_FROM_POOL = 4095` bytes on 4 KiB pages). Every request under that cap aligns `d.last` to 16 bytes and advances it in `O(1)` time with zero syscalls.",
  "ch5.p3": "When the current block fills up, `ngx_palloc_block` appends another block via `d.next` (carrying a slim 32-byte `ngx_pool_data_t` header); after an earlier block fails 4 allocations in a row, `pool->current` advances past it so future allocations skip full blocks. Anything larger than `pool->max` falls straight through to `malloc()` on the `pool->large` list. When the request finishes in `LOG`, one `ngx_destroy_pool()` runs cleanup callbacks and frees every block and large node together—at default settings (`12 × 512 B`), `2` blocks take only `2` free operations instead of 12. Meanwhile, outgoing bodies are chained via `ngx_chain_t` nodes pointing to scatter-gather `ngx_buf_t` slices in memory or file descriptors, avoiding monolithic response copies.",
  "dg.pool.cap": "Figure 5. ngx_pool_t layout: 80 B header, 16-byte-aligned bump pointer d.last, overflow d.next blocks, and oversized mallocs on pool->large.",
  "dg.pool.aria": "Diagram: top 4 KiB block with 80 B violet header, gold bumped allocations and free tail; arrow d.next to 32 B-headed block 1; red arrow pool->large to oversized malloc box",
