  "ch6.say.smooth": "Weights ({wa}, {wb}, {wc}) form a period of {per}: Nginx SWRR outputs <code>{seq}</code>, capping A's max streak at <em>{streak}</em> (vs {naiveStreak} naive) while keeping sum(cw) = 0 every step.",
  "ch6.say.naive": "<em>Naive WRR burst</em>: across {per} requests, dispatches <em>{streak} consecutive requests</em> to A before ever touching B or C, hammering the primary backend in bursts.",
  "ch6.say.recover": "<em>Fault recovery mode</em>: A's <code>effective_weight</code> drops by half and ramps +1 per selection back toward {wa}, yielding <code>{seq}</code> so a recovering node isn't overwhelmed immediately.",
  "ch6.limit": "The model leaves out: full circuit-breaking via `max_fails` and `fail_timeout`, `backup` server pools, and worker-local isolation when upstream HTTP keepalive pools cause each worker to run its own independent SWRR state.",
  "ch7.title": "Rate limiting (`limit_req`): `rate`, `burst`, and `nodelay` in the leaky bucket",
  "toc.ch7": "limit_req leaky bucket",
  "ch7.law": "`limit_req` is not a counter that resets on integer seconds—it is a millisecond-resolution leaky bucket inside a shared-memory red-black tree, capping excess to `burst` before emitting `503`.",
  "ch7.p1": "Boundary counters resetting once per second let clients send double bursts straddling `:00.99` and `:01.01`. In `src/http/modules/ngx_http_limit_req_module.c`, `ngx_http_limit_req_lookup` evaluates continuous integer-scaled leaky-bucket math where 1 request equals `1,000`:",
  "ch7.p2": "`excess = lr->excess - ctx->rate * ms / 1000 + 1000`",
  "ch7.p3": "Here `lr->excess` is leftover bucket depth from the previous accepted call (initialized to `0` on the first request), `ms` is elapsed milliseconds since `lr->last`, and `rate` is pre-scaled by `1,000`. If `excess` exceeds `burst * 1000`, Nginx returns `503` (`NGX_BUSY`)—and critically <strong>does not update `lr->excess` or `lr->last`</strong> so rejected floods never inflate bucket drain times. For accepted requests: without `nodelay`, the worker delays forwarding by `excess / rate` ms to pace traffic evenly; with `nodelay`, all requests fitting inside `burst` pass immediately while charging the bucket so future excess drains gradually.",
  "ch7.p4": "With defaults (`rate = 5 r/s`, `burst = 4`, 8 requests arriving 20 ms apart): request 1 (`excess = 0`) passes immediately, requests 2–5 fill the bucket up to `3,600` (`4` requests queued up to `720` ms without `nodelay`), and requests 6–8 overflow past `4,000` for `3` rejections (`503`). Toggling `nodelay` sends the first `5` immediately while still rejecting the exact same 3 overflow requests. Because the tree and LRU list live in `ngx_shm_zone_t` shared memory guarded by an atomic spinlock, all 4 workers share a single bucket.",
  "dg.limit.cap": "Figure 7. Shared-memory leaky bucket in ngx_http_limit_req_module: overflow past burst triggers 503 without raising bucket level.",
  "dg.limit.aria": "Diagram: cyan bucket with gold dashed burst limit, excess water inside, green leak arrow at rate*ms/1000, red 503 overflow exit, and violet shared-memory rbtree banner below",
  "dg.limit.burst": "burst × 1000 cap",
  "dg.limit.excess": "Bucket level lr->excess",
  "dg.limit.leak": "drain rate*ms/1000",
  "dg.limit.shm": "Shared-memory rbtree across all workers",
  "dg.limit.eq": "new = old - rate*ms/1000 + 1000",
