  "ch8.cv.aria": "Live red-black binary search tree maintaining black root, no red-red edges, and uniform black height, highlighting the leftmost minimum node in gold",
  "ch8.cv.empty": "All timers expired: epoll_wait passes -1 to sleep until a packet arrives",
  "ch8.cv.lazy": "Lazy skip rate",
  "ch8.leg.red": "Red node",
  "ch8.leg.blk": "Black node",
  "ch8.leg.min": "Leftmost min (sets timeout)",
  "ch8.read.act": "Active timers in tree",
  "ch8.read.exp": "Expired & popped",
  "ch8.read.wait": "Next epoll_wait timeout",
  "ch8.read.depth": "Tree depth / RB bound",
  "ch8.say.waiting": "At {now} ms (none expired yet): {act} active timers (depth {depth}) sit in the tree; minimum {minK} ms caps <code>epoll_wait</code> sleep at <em>{wait} ms</em> while lazy updates skip {skip}% of churn.",
  "ch8.say.expired": "At {now} ms, <em>{exp} expired timers</em> were popped from the left; {act} remain (depth {depth}), pushing the minimum to {minK} ms so <code>epoll_wait</code> waits <em>{wait} ms</em> (lazy skip {skip}%).",
  "ch8.say.empty": "At {now} ms all timers are cleared; with an empty tree, <code>epoll_wait</code> sleeps indefinitely (-1) until the next network interrupt (lazy skip {skip}%).",
  "ch8.limit": "The model leaves out: Nginx's cached timestamp optimization (`ngx_time_update` / `timer_resolution`) that avoids repeated `gettimeofday` syscalls, and shutdown drains when thousands of Keep-Alive deadlines fire on the exact same millisecond.",
  "ch9.title": "Zero-copy `sendfile()` and reverse-proxy `proxy_buffering`",
  "toc.ch9": "sendfile zero-copy & proxy_buffering",
  "ch9.law": "When serving static files, Nginx uses `sendfile()` to DMA bytes straight from the kernel page cache to the NIC, bypassing user space; yet when proxying a fast backend to a slow mobile client, Nginx intentionally buffers bytes in user space and temp files so slow clients never stall upstream workers.",
  "ch9.p1": "Opposite as those two paths appear, both obey the same accounting rule: <strong>keep slow peers out of scarce execution slots, and never copy bytes the CPU does not need to touch</strong>.",
  "ch9.p2": "<strong>1. Static files: `sendfile()` + `TCP_CORK` (zero user-space copies).</strong> Sending a 16 MiB file via classic 32 KiB `read()` + `write()` loops triggers `1,024` syscalls and `2,048` context switches while the CPU manually copies `32 MiB` twice across the user/kernel boundary. Enabling `sendfile on;` with `tcp_nopush on;` (`TCP_CORK` coalescing full MSS frames) finishes in `8` `sendfile` calls with <strong>0 MiB CPU-copied</strong>—a Scatter-Gather NIC pulls pages straight from Linux's page cache via DMA. One crucial caveat: standard user-space OpenSSL HTTPS cannot use `sendfile()` unless kernel TLS (`kTLS` `SSL_sendfile`) is enabled, silently falling back to user-space encryption buffers.",
  "ch9.p3": "<strong>2. Reverse proxy: `proxy_buffering on` (protecting fast backends from slow 3G clients).</strong> Suppose your 1 Gbps backend produces a 16 MiB payload in `134.2` ms, while a 2 Mbps cellular client needs `67.1` seconds to download it. With `proxy_buffering off;`, Nginx locks the backend worker open for the full `67.1` seconds waiting for client ACKs. With `proxy_buffering on;` (default), Nginx drains the backend in `134.2` ms into `proxy_buffers` and temp disk, releasing the backend `500`× sooner while trickling bytes to the client inside the async event loop.",
  "dg.sendfile.cap": "Figure 9. read()+write() crossing user space vs sendfile() DMA straight from kernel page cache to NIC (and the user-space TLS fallback caveat).",
  "dg.sendfile.aria": "Diagram: top red read+write crossing Disk, Page, User, NIC; bottom green sendfile jumping directly from Page to NIC without touching User; gold bottom note on kTLS requirement for HTTPS",
  "dg.sendfile.rw1": "read() + write(): 4 switches · 2× CPU copy",
  "dg.sendfile.rw2": "CPU copies bytes via user space",
  "dg.sendfile.sf1": "sendfile() + TCP_CORK: 0× CPU copy",
  "dg.sendfile.sf2": "Page cache -> Scatter-Gather NIC DMA",
  "dg.sendfile.tls": "Note: HTTPS without kTLS falls back",
  "ch9.model.title": "sendfile zero-copy & proxy_buffering decoupling lab",
  "ch9.model.how": "Switch between static file serving and reverse-proxy buffering, then uncheck the optimization toggle to compare CPU copies, syscalls, and how long a slow client holds a backend worker hostage.",
  "ch9.ctl.scen": "Workload scenario",
